 "verdict": "SIGNIFICANT ONLY AT MODEL LEVEL, AND THAT IS NOT ENOUGH: rho=+0.575 (p=0.0277, n=15) but family-level rho=+0.483 (p=0.1893, 9 families). Six of the correlated points are Pythia SIZES -- one corpus, tokenizer and recipe -- so the model-level test counts them six times. Collapsing them keeps the direction and loses the significance, which means the effect is real-looking but NOT established. About 21 independent families would settle it. SEPARATELY: the attractor BINARY carries no predictive value -- concentrating and non-concentrating models differ by only 0.063 in rep_4, permutation p=0.4055. Whatever signal exists is in T* as a graded quantity, not in whether a model concentrates.",
